{"latest_judgment":{"docno":null,"relevance":0,"reason":"The previous query returned no results, so there is no document to judge.","learned":"The perspective/viewpoint query was too restrictive or did not match available passages."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"dame van winkle\" (+ shrew termagant nagging) (+ rip lazy idleness neglect))","reason":"Search for criticism that connects the negative 'shrew' or nagging depiction of Dame Van Winkle to Rip's laziness and neglect, which is likely direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"dame van winkle\" (+ shrew termagant nagging) (+ rip lazy idleness neglect))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_795508142#13_1606539168","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3609","rank":1,"snippet":"Dutcher Peter Vanderdonk The Self-Important Man Unlock This Study Guide Now \" , \"segment\": \"Most of the older villagers believe Rip's story and credit the existence of Hudson's spirit. Even the younger villagers, who are more skeptical, begin to like Rip. Rip’s story later serves as a comfort to all the men who want to escape their nagging wives. The men are said to wish for a sip of the mystical beer Rip tasted and find themselves freed by a long and deep sleep. Dame Van Winkle<cover>Dame Van Winkle is Rip Van Winkle’s nagging </cover>wife. Rip often tries to escape her contentious nature by spending time with the men outside the inn. He also escapes her by going hunting and fishing. Dame Van Winkle is portrayed as a villain, deserving of blame. Yet, Dame Van Winkle is justified in her nagging, given that Rip won't work the farm or help with domestic matters. Rip leaves Dame Van Winkle alone to fend for the family.\" , \"start_char\": 5446.000000 , \"title\": \"Rip Van Winkle Characters - eNotes.com\" ,"}}
