{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_795508142#15_1606541909","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly supports the answer by explaining that the label “virago” marks Dame Van Winkle as domineering but can also suggest a warrior woman fighting against Rip to help herself and her family; it further notes that Rip still refuses to help her and escapes by hunting.","learned":"Dame Van Winkle's negative “virago” characterization can be read as a distorted version of justified struggle: she fights because Rip will not help support the family."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"dame van winkle\" (+ one-dimensional voiceless silence) (+ shrew virago termagant oppression))","reason":"Search for more passages explaining her negative portrayal as a one-dimensional, voiceless shrew/virago who functions as a symbol of oppression rather than a rounded character."}}
