{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_278218781#0_564828740","relevance":3,"reason":"The Owl Eyes character-analysis passage directly explains Dame Van Winkle's negative portrayal: Rip's refusal to work his farm creates marital conflict, and she is described in unflattering terms such as “shrew,” “virago,” and “termagant” because she lectures him about failing to work and provide for the family.","learned":"Owl Eyes reiterates that Dame Van Winkle's harsh negative labels are tied to Rip's failure to work his own farm and provide for his family."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3626","reason":"This query found direct evidence using the alternate phrase “Rip's wife,” so continuing may find additional unjudged analyses that do not use Dame Van Winkle's name prominently."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3626"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_20_1480048322#3_3235883976","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3626","rank":2,"snippet":"Winkle Written by Washington Irving Symbolism of Rip Van Winkle Written by Washington Irving Remember: This is just a sample from a fellow student. 100% plagiarism-free \" , \"segment\": \"In Irvin’s story, <cover>Rip Van Winkle, a man who wandered into the mountains after listening to the ongoing nagging of his wife, experiences strange encounters. He comes upon men, said to be the spirits of Hudson’s crew, who are playing a game of nine pins, and after drinking, he lies under a tree and goes to sleep. He wakes up twenty years later. Because he was use to what the world was like before the Revolutionary War of the United States, he notices how things changed drastically. Irvin uses the characters Rip to represent America, Dame to represent Britain, and the townspeople to represent the American people, to show the reader how the various parties participated in the American Revolution. Rip’s wife calls him lazy </cover>because he does nothing around their home. He would rather go hunting with his dog or go to the village and gossip with men. Everyone in the village loves Rip. The narrator states, “The children of the village, "}}
