{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_590209096#13_1318602144","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage from the original story provides direct textual evidence for why Dame Van Winkle is portrayed as nagging and harsh: she continually scolds Rip because of his idleness, carelessness, and the ruin he is bringing on the family.","learned":"The primary text itself grounds Dame Van Winkle's negative portrayal in Rip's irresponsible behavior, especially his refusal to work and the harm this causes his family."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3632","reason":"The quotation-based query is likely to retrieve more primary-text or analysis passages centered on the exact evidence explaining Dame Van Winkle's harsh treatment of Rip."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3632"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_110596411#1_278976942","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3632","rank":2,"snippet":"internal critique either. One such example of this internal American critique can be found in Washington Irving’s, “Rip Van Winkle.” Therefore, the purpose of this brief work is to discuss the theme of Irving’s timeless masterpiece and offer critical insight as to its deeper meanings. The tale of Rip Van Winkle is set in the whimsical wilderness of the Catskill Mountains in pre-revolutionary America. As the story portrays, Rip is a simple man caught between his own ferocious hedonistic motivations, and the insurmountable demands of his condescending and oppressive wife (Perkins). Stating, “His <cover>wife kept continually dinning in his ears about his idleness, his carelessness, and the ruin he was bringing on his family. </cover>Morning, noon, and night, her tongue was incessantly going...” (Perkins, 199), Irving provides the reader with an archetype antagonist in Rip’s wife Dame. Indeed, it is here that Rip’s primary motivation is developed.\" , \"start_char\": 720.000000 , \"title\": \"The True Meaning of Rip Van Winkle - Term Paper\" , \"url\": \"https://www.termpaperwarehouse.com/essay-on/The-True-Meaning-Of-Rip-Van/143756\" }"}}
