{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_278218781#6_564838526","relevance":3,"reason":"The Owl Eyes annotation directly supports an interpretive answer: Rip fears Dame Van Winkle because she represents the return from forest freedom to work, domestic demands, and village commercialism; another note frames his relief at her death as a cynical form of liberty. This helps explain why she is portrayed as an oppressive, negative force rather than a rounded spouse.","learned":"Owl Eyes offers another reason for the negative portrayal: Dame Van Winkle embodies the demands of work, domestic responsibility, and social/commercial order that Rip wants to escape."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3635","reason":"The exact-phrase Owl Eyes query is producing direct critical annotations about Dame Van Winkle's negative portrayal, so continue to retrieve any further unjudged passages from the same focused line of evidence."}}
